xmodel/block-descent-a1-quartic-cycle1-kummer-collision-hostile-review-gpt55-20260830.log:2151:+### 4.3 Two distinct roots, not simple roots
xmodel/block-descent-a1-quartic-cycle1-kummer-collision-hostile-review-gpt55-20260830.log:2162:+The multiplicity of a root of `S(0,y)` is the local intersection multiplicity of the retained branch with the residual branch or branches. The charged inputs give immersion of the normalization, not transversality of distinct branches. Therefore the stronger statement "the two roots are simple" is REFUTED.
xmodel/block-descent-a1-quartic-cycle1-kummer-collision-hostile-review-gpt55-20260830.log:2393:+Connectedness/product issue: `pi^{-1}(V)` is a nonempty open of irreducible `U`; it is irreducible. The finite etale algebra is therefore a domain here, not a product of disconnected global components. After strict henselization or completion it can split as a product, but that is a local splitting used later and does not alter the global field argument.
xmodel/block-descent-a1-quartic-cycle1-kummer-collision-hostile-review-gpt55-20260830.log:2457:+At a self-identification point of `C0` outside `B`, the finite etale quartic algebra becomes split after completed strict henselization. On the two selected sheets corresponding to the two branches of `C0`, choose local reduced branch equations `u=0` and `v=0`. The divisor condition gives, after absorbing units by completed-local `mu`-th roots,
xmodel/block-descent-a1-quartic-cycle1-kummer-collision-hostile-review-gpt55-20260830.log:2611:+The discriminant support is `uv=0`. The origin has geometric fibre lengths `(2,2)`. A generic point of either discriminant branch gives one double point and two simple points after geometric splitting, i.e. `(2,1,1)`. Both factors are regular.
xmodel/block-descent-a1-quartic-cycle1-kummer-collision-hostile-review-gpt55-20260830.log:2653:+This does not force the conductor to be the unit ideal and does not force the monogenic order to be normal. The split local model explicitly has trivial normalized canonical module and a nonempty square index divisor.
xmodel/block-descent-a1-quartic-cycle1-kummer-collision-hostile-review-gpt55-20260830.log:2720:+S(0,y) has two distinct roots: CONFIRMED.
xmodel/block-descent-a1-quartic-cycle1-kummer-collision-hostile-review-gpt55-20260830.log:2721:+those roots are simple: REFUTED.
xmodel/block-descent-a1-quartic-cycle1-kummer-collision-hostile-review-gpt55-20260830.log:2752:+on C0 gives two distinct roots of S(0,y), not necessarily simple roots.
