xmodel/sol-symplectic.log:5076:xmodel/sol-approaches.log:7654:./SHEET6.md:20:- **The 4→5 explosion is NOT case count — it is a provable method failure.** Egorov 2002 constructed a proper 5-sheeted "exotic covering" of C² (branching along two planes F₁*,F₂*; H²_c ≅ Z²; S₁² = −3, S₂² = −15, S₁·S₂ = −3; K(S₁)=K(S₂)=1) consistent with EVERY quasi-topological invariant Domrina–Orevkov use. So 5 required a new analytic layer: both 2008 proofs inject Abhyankar approximate-root/Puiseux-coefficient arithmetic (pole orders of g along f-branches; jac=1 exponent identities) that sees strictly more than the topology. That is the conceptual jump; the case tree itself only grew ~2×.
xmodel/sol-symplectic.log:5086:xmodel/sol-approaches.log:7761:./xmodel/grok-thmB-review.md:122:- Even before §7, identifying (9) with “exactly” the campaign L42 quadratic overclaims the overlap. Campaign \(F_{10}\) is a specific \(K\)-linear image of six scalars (Galois-twisted through-\(d_0\) products). Sol’s \(r\) is a Hensel lift of a square root of \(cs\) in \(K[\eta]/(p)\), degree \(<18\) after two steps, not proved to lie in that 6-plane. The actual residue-A skeleton also carries \(w_i\) at level 37 (slot 5) and \(vf\) at 34/36 (slots 2,4); family (9) has those slots empty. So (9) is a flexibility in a *larger* ambient space than the campaign jets. Harmless for B-O as stated; another reason the family is not a residue-A witness.
