xmodel/sol-symplectic.log:12831:xmodel/codex-eval-20260812.md-3048-SHEET6.md:15:- **(B) Sigray's decorated Eggers–Wall trees** (verbatim, thesis in hand): Abhyankar normal form (§2: Newton polygons in rectangles, fibers with 2 points at infinity); Eggers–Wall tree of Puiseux expansions of f-fiber branches at infinity (§3); extended Abhyankar fundamental theorem + partial order (§4); td and pole orders of g computed from tree data (§5, Props 5.5/5.8); tree structure symbols ↘↗ (§6); finite critical values budget (§7, Cor 7.1); Jacobian-forced number theory linking neighboring vertices (§8, Prop 8.1); §9: decoration Q(F) = (D_F, deg p_F, ν_F, M_F, κ_F(1−π(F))) must propagate along the "characteristic sequence" F₀→…→(0,y) to the root, under Diophantine constraints, with budget Σλ_{F_i} ≤ td−2 (St 9.4) — and no admissible propagation survives for td ≤ 5.
xmodel/sol-symplectic.log:12833:xmodel/codex-eval-20260812.md-3050-SHEET6.md:20:- **The 4→5 explosion is NOT case count — it is a provable method failure.** Egorov 2002 constructed a proper 5-sheeted "exotic covering" of C² (branching along two planes F₁*,F₂*; H²_c ≅ Z²; S₁² = −3, S₂² = −15, S₁·S₂ = −3; K(S₁)=K(S₂)=1) consistent with EVERY quasi-topological invariant Domrina–Orevkov use. So 5 required a new analytic layer: both 2008 proofs inject Abhyankar approximate-root/Puiseux-coefficient arithmetic (pole orders of g along f-branches; jac=1 exponent identities) that sees strictly more than the topology. That is the conceptual jump; the case tree itself only grew ~2×.
xmodel/sol-symplectic.log:12835:xmodel/codex-eval-20260812.md-3052-SHEET6.md:25:- **Configuration layer**: ramification budget Σλ ≤ td−2 = 4 and multi-pole-vertex splits (partitions of td−1 = 5 over dicritical vertices: 7 shapes) ⇒ **~7 top-level branches** vs effectively 3–4 at td 5.
