xmodel/k16-t6-sol56-20260903.log:168056:+The charged generic forms place every high pivot in `Q(t)[y]/(H_t)`. After clearing the displayed finite rational denominators, their recurrences have integral `Z[t,y]` representatives. With `d=2(2t+1)y-(t+1)`, the visible primitive linear classes are
xmodel/k16-t6-sol56-20260903.log:168070:+Res(H_t,L_C)=-4(2t+1)^2(3t+2)^3 F_C(t,j),
xmodel/k16-t6-sol56-20260903.log:168071:+Res(H_t,L_Q)=-4(2t+1)^2(t+1)(3t+2)^2 F_Q(t,j),
xmodel/k16-t6-sol56-20260903.log:168072:+Res(H_t,L_2)=-12(2t+1)^2(t+1)(3t+2)(4t+1),
xmodel/k16-t6-sol56-20260903.log:168075:+with `F_C(t,t-n)` a coefficientwise-positive quartic for `n>=1` and `F_Q(t,2t+1-n)=n^2+4nt+6n+4t^2-3 >= 12`. Hence all reconstruction and high pivots are units, including on split fibres. At `t=6`, `t6_laurent_pivot_specialization.json` records all 13 specialized high coefficients and nonzero resultants.
xmodel/k16-t6-sol56-20260903.log:168140:+No terminal `sat()` call is hidden here. Since `cbar` is already proved a unit and eliminated, the drivers directly test the terminal ideal. Modular rings contain raw `H=4H_6` as a generator and the exact ring uses `minpoly=H`; because 4 is a unit in `Q` and in all three odd characteristics, these define the same quotient as primitive `H_6`. Two primes split `H_6` and one leaves it irreducible, and all three avoid the leading coefficient/discriminant and every Q/A/unit denominator or resultant.
